WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:02.245
Welcome.

2
00:00:02.995 --> 00:00:09.673
This first video gives you the big picture of
the job, before we look at any single cable.

3
00:00:11.023 --> 00:00:18.287
We will cover what this hall is for, the three
jobs your cables do, the five cable paths you

4
00:00:18.287 --> 00:00:23.972
install, what you never touch, and one rule
that beats every other rule.

5
00:00:28.122 --> 00:00:31.919
A data hall is one large room full of racks.

6
00:00:32.669 --> 00:00:37.619
A rack is a tall metal cabinet that holds
computer equipment.

7
00:00:38.969 --> 00:00:43.875
Most of the racks in Data Hall 4 are called
TPU racks.

8
00:00:44.625 --> 00:00:48.145
TPU stands for Tensor Processing Unit.

9
00:00:48.895 --> 00:00:56.784
It is a computer chip built for artificial
intelligence work, and one TPU rack holds many

10
00:00:56.784 --> 00:00:57.493
of them.

11
00:00:59.093 --> 00:01:01.695
One rack on its own is powerful.

12
00:01:02.445 --> 00:01:08.099
But the real work happens when hundreds of
racks act as one big machine.

13
00:01:09.449 --> 00:01:12.371
Your cables are how they work together.

14
00:01:13.121 --> 00:01:19.991
Every cable you install connects one piece of
equipment to another, on an exact port.

15
00:01:24.141 --> 00:01:27.042
Here is the whole job on one page.

16
00:01:27.792 --> 00:01:31.333
It comes from page four of the installation
guide.

17
00:01:32.083 --> 00:01:34.238
Take a moment to look at it.

18
00:01:36.238 --> 00:01:39.438
Your cables build three separate systems.

19
00:01:40.188 --> 00:01:42.684
The first is the data network.

20
00:01:43.434 --> 00:01:47.978
The guide calls it the DCN, for Data Center
Network.

21
00:01:50.278 --> 00:01:58.256
Data leaves each TPU rack on one thick fiber
cable, goes to the rack at the end of the row,

22
00:01:58.256 --> 00:02:03.078
then on to patch panels and big switches
called spines.

23
00:02:04.428 --> 00:02:08.375
The second system is the optical switch
fabric.

24
00:02:09.125 --> 00:02:16.827
Each TPU rack sends three cables to three
special racks called SPOCS racks, which hold

25
00:02:16.827 --> 00:02:19.066
optical circuit switches.

26
00:02:20.416 --> 00:02:23.893
The third system is the management network.

27
00:02:24.643 --> 00:02:31.043
It is a smaller, separate network that people
use to set up and watch the equipment.

28
00:02:33.343 --> 00:02:35.157
Why keep them separate?

29
00:02:35.907 --> 00:02:38.253
Each one has a different job.

30
00:02:39.003 --> 00:02:45.576
And if the main data network has a problem,
the management network is still there to reach

31
00:02:45.576 --> 00:02:46.598
the equipment.

32
00:02:50.748 --> 00:02:55.143
Now let us name the five cable paths your crew
installs.

33
00:02:55.893 --> 00:02:57.855
We will go one at a time.

34
00:02:59.255 --> 00:03:00.429
Path one.

35
00:03:01.179 --> 00:03:08.027
One 144-fiber trunk from each TPU rack to its
end of row rack.

36
00:03:09.377 --> 00:03:10.614
Path two.

37
00:03:11.364 --> 00:03:20.008
Eight 192-fiber trunks from each end of row
rack to the DCN patch panels, and short cords

38
00:03:20.008 --> 00:03:22.436
from there to the spines.

39
00:03:23.786 --> 00:03:25.087
Path three.

40
00:03:25.837 --> 00:03:33.005
Three OCS cables from each TPU rack, one to
each of its three SPOCS racks.

41
00:03:34.355 --> 00:03:35.635
Path four.

42
00:03:36.385 --> 00:03:41.868
Six copper cables from each TPU rack to its
end of row rack.

43
00:03:43.218 --> 00:03:44.562
Path five.

44
00:03:45.312 --> 00:03:52.731
Management trunks from each end of row rack to
the management spines, plus management cords

45
00:03:52.731 --> 00:03:54.443
from the SPOCS racks.

46
00:03:55.793 --> 00:03:57.115
Five paths.

47
00:03:57.865 --> 00:04:04.031
Every lesson after this one follows one of
these paths from its start to its finish.

48
00:04:08.181 --> 00:04:11.637
Just as important is what you do not touch.

49
00:04:12.437 --> 00:04:17.877
Anything inside the TPU rack is already cabled
when it arrives.

50
00:04:18.627 --> 00:04:24.771
That includes the machines, the trays, the
short internal cables, and the batteries.

51
00:04:25.521 --> 00:04:27.078
Leave them alone.

52
00:04:28.428 --> 00:04:30.711
And your work ends at the spines.

53
00:04:31.461 --> 00:04:35.066
Anything past the spines belongs to someone
else.

54
00:04:36.416 --> 00:04:39.104
Here is a simple test from the guide.

55
00:04:39.854 --> 00:04:43.993
If a cable never leaves the rack, it is not
yours.

56
00:04:48.393 --> 00:04:51.827
Finally, the rule that beats every other rule.

57
00:04:52.577 --> 00:04:56.716
Your site's cutsheet is the final word on any
port.

58
00:04:58.116 --> 00:05:04.196
The cutsheet is the spreadsheet that lists
every cable and every stop it makes.

59
00:05:05.546 --> 00:05:12.266
If a label, the guide, or this course ever
disagrees with the cutsheet, do not guess.

60
00:05:13.016 --> 00:05:14.936
Stop and ask your lead.

61
00:05:16.286 --> 00:05:22.750
This course teaches you the patterns, so you
understand why each cable goes where it goes.

62
00:05:23.500 --> 00:05:26.231
The cutsheet tells you exactly where.

63
00:05:30.381 --> 00:05:32.343
That is the big picture.

64
00:05:33.093 --> 00:05:40.353
Next, open lesson one in the course to explore
the diagram yourself, match each cable to

65
00:05:40.353 --> 00:05:43.653
where it goes, and take the short check.

